<!--Post: 4974--><!--Time: 1106843271-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4974"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tippy<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tippy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 8:27 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Teardrop - you can&#39;t hide from your provider - but you can hide from sites you are visiting.  Providers, as far as I know, do not monitor their customer&#39;s activities - without a reason - they just don&#39;t have the manpower to do so.  But, if a site one of their customers is visiting tracks that customer back to the provider - that provider is required, by law, if subpoenaed to hand over the records of the customer. <BR> <BR>So basically, if you want to remain anonymous use a proxy server.  That sounds hard - but it isn&#39;t.  For example, a good one is Steganos Anonmyous Pro.  Just plain ole&#39; software - easy to install and it runs in the background - camoflaging your IP and telling the sites you are visiting that your computer is in Old Mexico, the Netherlands, or one of numerous places. <BR> <BR>The site, then, cannot trace you - which means your provider will not be contacted. <BR> <BR>I, personally, think it is more hassle than it is worth - unless you think someone is going to stalk you -or you like to pretend you are fourteen different posters on one forum. <BR> <BR>BTK is taking a chance no matter where he is - as all net activity is trackable at &#34;some&#34; level.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
