<!--Post: 5600--><!--Time: 1107214255-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5600"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 3:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I have to disagree with you on a point or two kerri: <BR> <BR>Your Pastor was talking about vigilante justice not a court trial and sentance. <BR> <BR>The police and FBI do not judge criminals they investagate and or arrest them. <BR> <BR>We serve as jurors and therefore &#40;not always as in a trial by judge&#41; do in fact judge citizens criminal actions and as in the Scott Peterson case can also bestow a judgement of death on a convicted criminal. <BR> <BR>Judge not lest ye be judged; The world often takes this verse out of context and uses it to accuse Christians of being &#34;judgmental&#34; when they speak of sin. In the context of the verse Jesus is telling His disciples not to judge one another, something the Bible condemns &#40;Romans 14:10; James 4:11&#41;. In Luke 6:41,42 He speaks of seeing a speck in a brother’s eye. In John 7:24 He said, &#34;Judge not according to the appearance, but judge righteous judgment.&#34; If someone steals, lies, commits adultery or murder, etc., the Christian can make a &#40;righteous&#41; moral judgment and say that the actions were morally wrong, and that these sins will have eternal consequences. <BR> <BR>The basis of our US Court system is a biblical one and we are in fact ordained to judge whether it be appointed, elected or serving as a juror. <BR> <BR>This is why I mentioned to the poster &#34;Jesus Saves&#34; that he or she should not presume BTKs place with God but that it is OK to point out to BTK that his actions have eternal consquences, as well as, what most of us hope he will face here on Earth... Justice. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
